```

So after parsing an MQTT5 packet, it iterates over properties with `mg_mqtt_next_prop`, and then calls `mg_crc32(0, prop.val.buf, prop.val.len)`.

The vulnerability is in `mg_mqtt_next_prop` for `MQTT_PROP_TYPE_BINARY_DATA`:
```c
case MQTT_PROP_TYPE_BINARY_DATA:
    if (i + 2 > end) return 0;
    prop->val.len = (uint16_t) ((((uint16_t) i[0]) << 8) | i[1]);
    prop->val.buf = (char *) i + 2;
    new_pos += 2 + prop->val.len;
    break;
```

Notice that for `MQTT_PROP_TYPE_BINARY_DATA`, there is NO check that `i + 2 + prop->val.len > end` (unlike `MQTT_PROP_TYPE_STRING` and `MQTT_PROP_TYPE_STRING_PAIR` which have that check). So `prop->val.len` can be set to a value larger than the remaining bytes, and `prop->val.buf` points to `i + 2`. Then when the harness calls `mg_crc32(0, prop.val.buf, prop.val.len)`, it reads `prop->val.len` bytes starting from `prop->val.buf`, which goes beyond the buffer → heap buffer over-read.

So the root cause is the missing check `if (i + 2 + prop->val.len > end) return 0;` in the BINARY_DATA case. The safety obligation that should have held: `i + 2 + prop->val.len <= end` (i.e., the declared length must not exceed the remaining packet).

